	"...the time needed ...as if done step by step..."  The
current implementation requires unpacking nested containers step by
step, yes.  This is not because it is reasonable, and not because
game balance requires it, but because your better way of doing it was
either harder to implement or was not obvious.

	Reasonable:  try nesting ten plastic bags inside each other.
Now reach into the center one.  It's quick and easy:  you automatic-
ally lined up the openings of all of the bags as you nested them, so
it's no problem at all.  In game terms, it takes one turn, not twenty
one.

	Game balance:  this isn't an issue.  By the time you're nest-
ing containers, you're normally at the castle or later.  It's possible
to die because fiddling with your inventory took too long, yes, but 
that really isn't a common cause of death.

	So far, who cares?  But there is an issue with "...it freezes
you in the process...".

	Some multi-turn actions can't be interrupted.  If a monster
comes up, you are quite possibly dead.  If you die, it clearly isn't 
a well-deserved YASD.  Instead, it feels stupid and arbitrary.  This
makes these multi-turn freeze-ups a flaw instead of a feature.  See
appendix 1 for a discussion.

	I suggest that you make nested looting take one turn.

	(As a side benefit, this eliminates concerns of players
becoming overtaxed part way through the unpack / repack actions.)



APPENDIX 1:  MULTI-TURN FREEZE-UPS

1.	Donning armor.  You take off your leather armor, then start
putting on mithril.  A leocrotta shows up and kills you while you're
effectively paralysed.  

	In real life, you could ditch the partly-donned armor (at
a cost in time) or continue putting it on (at a cost in time) or
fight with it partly on (with the armor hindering you a lot more 
than it protects you).  In Nethack, the most *practical* way to
implement this was to shrug and just have the player paralyzed until
he finishes donning the armor.  That isn't the same as the *best* 
possible way to implement this.  I doubt that it's worth implementing
partially-donned armor, but it would eliminate one YA-Stupid-And-
Arbitrary-D.

2.	Praying.  A leocrotta has you at the brink of death.  You
start to pray, the prayer is clearly not successful, but you keep
mindlessly plugging away at it regardless.  YASAAD.

	Everyone in this fantasy world should know the signs of a
successful prayer.  When your prayer is being ignored, you would
give up on it and try something else.  "You start to pray.  The
leocrotta bites!  You stop praying, since Thoth is clearly ignoring 
you."  Instead, the game basically shoots you through the head.

	I don't recall any other freeze-ups.

------------------
	Dev Teamers:  when someone dies, you want them to say "gosh,
I was stupid", or "gosh, I didn't know that", or "gosh, this game
is hard", or "gosh, I was unlucky."  You don't want them to say
"gosh, this game is stupid and arbitrary".

	If you flinch at the idea of fewer YASAADs making the game
noticably easier, the early game is very easy to rebalance.  Here's
a (stupid) rebalance:  change "very low HPs" from a major problem to 
a minor problem and you change the prayer timeout correction for it
from 200 turns to 100 turns.  This would replace those YASAADs with 
an even greater number of conventional deaths.
------------------

	Nested looting would remove a bit of real-world tedium.  Good!
Unfortunately, if it involves freeze-ups, the rule would be:  "Don't
use the nested looting option; it can YASAAD you."  Be bored or be
dead.

	Multi-turn freeze-ups do not improve the game.  Having multi-
turn freeze-ups when using an improved #loot command does not improve
the game.  Seeing as how there is no real-world or game-play argument
for freeze-ups here, I recommend making the improved #loot command
take only one turn.

	(Dev Teamers:  I would also remove the #pray freeze-up, since
that's easy to fix.  Half-donned armor would be a pain to code; leave
it.)


